      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's an attempt to expand and refine the critical comments section:\n\nThe authors' claim of a significant improvement in time-series forecasting using the proposed TemporalTrans architecture is intriguing, but a closer examination of the empirical results reveals some inconsistencies. While the authors achieve competitive performance on the ETT and Weather datasets, their results often suffer from high variance, which makes it unclear whether these results can be generalized to other datasets. Furthermore, the authors' metric of evaluation is solely based on Mean Squared Error (MSE), which might not capture the nuances of long-range dependencies in time-series data.\n\nUpon closer inspection of the TemporalTrans architecture, it becomes apparent that the adaptive attention mechanism relies on a heuristic scaling approach that warrants further investigation. Specifically, the authors propose scaling the attention weights linearly with sequence length, which may not be feasible through standard techniques like complexity normalization or gradient clipping. Furthermore, the proposed scaling mechanism lacks clarity on how it mitigates potential vanishing gradients arising from deep neural networks. A more rigorous treatment of scaling would require examining the interplay between attention weights and sequence length in a more systematic manner, potentially leveraging techniques from inverse problems or graph-based methods.\n\nRegarding the relations to existing literature and prior work, the authors' claim of",
